		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Mining vehicle safety simulator, powered by Bullet]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=21985#p21985"><![CDATA[
Hi all,<br><br>My company, <a href="http://www.realspace3d.com" class="postlink">RealSpace</a>, has recently produced a simulation for <a href="http://www.teck.com" class="postlink">Teck</a>, to help train their vehicle operators on safety hazards. We used Bullet to power our vehicles and collisions.<br><br>The vehicle that operators are training on is a rubber-tired bulldozer: a 50,000 kg vehicle with fixed axis wheels and a two-part hinged chassis. We implemented it using two raycast vehicles, and a hinge constraint, and although we had to up the maximum suspension forces a little, Bullet was able to handle it extremely well. <br><br>You can read more, or see a video of the vehicle in action <a href="http://www.realspace3d.com/vehicle-safety-simulator-for-teck-mining-powered-by-bullet-physics" class="postlink">here</a>.<br><br><img src="http://www.realspace3d.com/wp-content/gallery/cache/198__640x480_realspace-bulletphysics-bulldozer-simulator.png" class="postimage" alt="Image"><br><br>I'd also like to thank everyone on the forums here who has answered my questions in the last year or so!<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=6215">dakffyd</a> — Wed Feb 02, 2011 10:32 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
